WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.040
Writing software often feels abstract, but rendering a user interface is bound by strict,

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.320
unyielding physical deadlines.

3
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:13.880
At 60 frames per second, a display refreshes every 16.67 milliseconds.

4
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.840
For a 120 hertz display, that shrinks to 8.33 milliseconds.

5
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:23.280
If the system takes a fraction of a millisecond longer to commit draw commands, the display

6
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.720
misses the update.

7
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.600
You drop a frame.

8
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:31.240
Writing this requires knowing exactly where your animation code executes, because the execution

9
00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.840
environment dictates the performance.

10
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.240
Hitting 120 frames per second requires strict adherence to the physics of the device's

11
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.840
refresh cycle.

12
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.480
Two threads dominate the React Native pipeline.

13
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.000
The JS thread runs the Hermes VM, executing all React renders, state updates, business logic,

14
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.480
and network callbacks.

15
00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:53.920
The native UI thread dispatches touch events, runs the layout engine, and draws pixels to

16
00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.400
the screen.

17
00:00:55.400 --> 00:01:00.320
Writing or mutating a native view must happen on the UI thread, which relies on the JS

18
00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.680
thread to deliver rendering instructions on time.

19
00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.680
Because there is exactly one JS thread per app, heavy business logic will block it.

20
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:14.400
When the JS thread chokes, the UI thread receives no updates, and the rendering pipeline stalls.

21
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.040
Consider a standard performance failure, an active, smoothly running animation suddenly

22
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.680
stutters because a heavy React state update fires at the same time.

23
00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.120
This flow chart maps the legacy React Native architecture, where these threads communicated

24
00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:32.840
exclusively through an asynchronous JSON message queue known as the bridge.

25
00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:38.600
An animation driven by the JS thread recalculated its value every frame, serialize that data,

26
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.640
and sent it across this bridge.

27
00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:46.080
At 60 frames per second, the system forces 60 serialized JSON-round trips every second,

28
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.160
just to keep the animation moving.

29
00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:54.440
These 60 updates compete for space in the exact same queue as heavy network responses and

30
00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.640
dense state updates.

31
00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.600
Congestion is inevitable.

32
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:04.000
Engineers mitigated this using the use Native driver API, sending the animation description

33
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.080
over the bridge exactly once, allowing the native UI thread to run the loop.

34
00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:14.400
However, this approach had measurable setup latency, and was strictly limited to declarative

35
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.480
up-front animations, making it useless for continuous, dynamically changing gesture inputs.

36
00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:27.200
A synchronous serialized communication cannot drive complex, interruptible interface animations.

37
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.720
Simply put, the architecture itself was the bottleneck.

38
00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.120
The new architecture solves this bottleneck.

39
00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.720
The bridge is completely replaced by the JavaScript interface, or JSI.

40
00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:43.680
JSI establishes direct C++ method bindings accessed natively by the Hermes VM.

41
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:49.320
This eliminates serialization, removes asynchronous waiting, and destroys the message queue.

42
00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:54.120
The framework shifts away from a disconnected message passing system to a synchronous showed

43
00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.640
memory model.

44
00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:01.160
By removing the bridge's latency, JSI allows the JSI and native layers to execute complex

45
00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:06.560
calculations across boundaries, well within a single 16 millisecond frame.

46
00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:12.280
Looking back at the UI thread, the reanimated library leverages JSI to spawn worklets, executing

47
00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:15.600
animation functions directly within the native environment.

48
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.920
A worklet is a secondary miniature Hermes runtime that lives directly on the UI thread to execute

49
00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:23.080
animation functions locally.

50
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:27.960
If we run that same heavy React state update alongside a gesture animation, but execute the

51
00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:33.280
animation logic inside a worklet, the main JSI thread can block for 30 milliseconds, failing

52
00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:37.960
its rendering deadline, while the animation continues executing at full frame rate.

53
00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:42.760
The worklet reads and writes shared value synchronously via JSI on the UI thread.

54
00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:46.640
It is completely isolated from the congested JSI event loop.

55
00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.440
Moving animation logic to the UI thread via worklets keeps the frame rate stable, even when

56
00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:55.000
the JavaScript thread is overwhelmed by application load.

57
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.480
JSI also changes how the framework handles layout measurements.

58
00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.120
The measure function now returns native layout coordinate synchronously.

59
00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:06.600
There is no more waiting for an asynchronous callback chain to locate a view on screen.

60
00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:11.160
This ties directly into fabric, React Native's concurrent renderer, which manages layout

61
00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.200
trees on the UI thread.

62
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:18.080
Fabric can intelligently interrupt low priority rendering tasks, like off screen list updates,

63
00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:21.640
to immediately process urgent gesture driven animations.

64
00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:26.760
Combined, JSI and fabric create an architecture that physically prioritizes human interaction

65
00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:28.600
over background compute.

66
00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:31.720
This matrix maps out the new architectural challenge.

67
00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:35.280
Engineers must explicitly manage how data travels between the main JSI thread and the

68
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.960
UI worklet.

69
00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:42.720
Shared values are the primary, lock-free mechanism for passing data between these threads without

70
00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.400
writing explicit synchronization code.

71
00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.840
A strict boundary rule applies.

72
00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:53.800
Never attempt to call a standard React state setter directly from inside a UI worklet.

73
00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:55.400
React isn't thread safe.

74
00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.600
State setters must execute in the main JS loop.

75
00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:03.920
To safely dispatch a function from a UI worklet to the main JS thread, wrap the call in run

76
00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:05.400
on JS.

77
00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:10.800
Conversely, to schedule a UI worklet function from JS, use run on UI.

78
00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:15.360
Treating these boundaries as rigid manual handoffs prevents dangerous race conditions and

79
00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.640
completely protects the UI thread from crashes.

80
00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:21.360
Managing the animation frame is only the first step.

81
00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:26.680
Heavy tasks executed immediately after an animation can still cause the trailing frames to stutter.

82
00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:30.920
This diagram shows the interaction manager, a holding queue designed to safely defer this

83
00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:32.840
non-essential work.

84
00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:39.040
The run after interactions method holds tasks in this queue until all active animations and

85
00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:41.400
touch events are fully complete.

86
00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.520
There's one crucial exception.

87
00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:48.960
Decorative or looping background animations must be flagged as is interaction false.

88
00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.360
Otherwise, they hold the queue open permanently and block deferred tasks.

89
00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:59.120
Finally, when verifying these optimizations, only profile thread performance in a fully compiled

90
00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:04.760
release build, development mode injects heavy JS overhead that generates inaccurate flame

91
00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:05.760
charts.

92
00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:10.560
Achieving 120 frames per second means abandoning the black box mentality.

93
00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:15.320
You have to map every single calculation to its correct thread and aggressively protect

94
00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:17.360
your 16 millisecond physical budget.

